**How to implement.** `OpticalConstructor.Ui.Tests` references the shared test-support project, so one compiled copy of the ground truth, the designs and the kit locator serves both test suites (001 §Q AT-2 'Both `BerremanTests` and `OpticalConstructor.Ui.Tests` reference it, so one compiled copy serves both test suites'; 001 §S10 'referenced by `BerremanTests` and `OpticalConstructor.Ui.Tests`'; the S10.2 brief). `Berreman/OpticalConstructor/OpticalConstructor.Ui.Tests/OpticalConstructor.Ui.Tests.fsproj` gains the project reference to `..\..\BerremanTestSupport\BerremanTestSupport.fsproj` after its last `ProjectReference`, the `OpticalConstructor.TestWindows.App` one (`OpticalConstructor.Ui.Tests.fsproj:268`), with no comment (AGENTS.md 'No new comments in `.fsproj`'). This step adds the reference 001 §Q AT-2 requires and changes no source file of the suite, so no `ui-tests` class holds a changed fact and that gate is not selected; what the reference can affect, the suite's build and load, is what `build` and `ui-smoke` prove. The reference brings no assembly besides `BerremanTestSupport` itself, because `OpticalConstructor.Domain` and `OpticalConstructor.Optimization` already reach the suite through `OpticalConstructor.Ui` (`OpticalConstructor.Ui.fsproj:176-178`). The test projects stay outside the elevation gate's scope (`Elevation/Run-ElevationGate.ps1:151`), so the gate analyzes nothing new here. No test and no package is added. `ui-smoke` runs whole (`whole_suite: light`, about 27 s) and shows that the headless host still loads, discovers and runs the suite with the new reference.

**Acceptance.** After the reference is added, `OpticalConstructor.Ui.Tests.fsproj` MUST reference `Berreman/BerremanTestSupport/BerremanTestSupport.fsproj`, so that `BerremanTests` and `OpticalConstructor.Ui.Tests` build against one `BerremanTestSupport` assembly. ui-smoke MUST pass, its count unchanged; and build, elevation and the twenty s34 invariants MUST pass.
